  <Section
    id="youtube-setup"
    theme="github-dark"
    label="YouTube, optional"
    class="f-mono"
    data-fx-none
  >
    <p class="eyebrow" data-reveal="fade">If you want the upload too</p>
    <h2 class="display" data-fx="typewriter" data-fx-speed="45">
      YouTube upload, optional.
    </h2>
    <p
      class="lede"
      data-reveal="rise"
      style="margin-inline:auto;max-width:44rem"
    >
      Uploading to YouTube is optional. Everything else in Studio works without
      it, and Pito never contacts YouTube until you set it up.
    </p>
    <p class="yt-why mono" data-reveal="rise">
      Why your own project? YouTube gives each project 10000 units of upload
      allowance a day, and one video costs 1600 of them — about 6 videos. If
      Pito shipped with one shared account, the first few people to upload each
      day would spend everyone else's allowance. Your own project means your
      allowance is yours, and your videos never queue behind a stranger's.
    </p>
    <ol class="yt-steps" data-reveal="rise">
      <li>
        <strong>Sign in to Google Cloud.</strong> Open the
        <a href="https://console.cloud.google.com/" class="pito-blue"
          >Google Cloud console</a
        > and sign in with the Google account that owns your YouTube channel. It is
        free, and you are not signing up for anything — the console is just where
        Google keeps the settings that let a program talk to your account.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Make a project.</strong> At the top of the page there is a project
        picker. Open it, choose
        <a
          href="https://console.cloud.google.com/projectcreate"
          class="pito-blue">New Project</a
        >, give it any name ("Pito" does fine) and create it. Wait for the
        picker to switch to your new project before going on.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Turn on YouTube Data API v3.</strong> Open
        <a
          href="https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/library/youtube.googleapis.com"
          class="pito-blue">the API page</a
        >, make sure your new project is the one selected at the top, and press
        Enable. This is the switch that lets anything upload on your behalf.
        Nothing is charged for it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Fill in the consent screen.</strong> Google asks who is asking for
        access. On
        <a
          href="https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials/consent"
          class="pito-blue">the consent screen</a
        >, choose External, put your own name and email in the boxes it marks
        required, and save. Then find "Test users" and add your own Google
        address. While the app is in testing only the accounts you list here can
        sign in — which is exactly what you want, because the only account that
        will ever use it is yours.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Create the credentials.</strong> Go to
        <a
          href="https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials"
          class="pito-blue">Credentials</a
        >, press Create Credentials, choose OAuth client ID, and pick Desktop
        app as the type. Google then shows you a client ID and a client secret.
        Leave that page open.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Paste them into Pito.</strong> Copy the client ID and the client secret
        from that page into the two boxes in Studio's doctor. They are stored on this
        machine only, readable by you alone, and you can remove them at any time.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sign in once.</strong> Pito opens a Google sign-in page and asks for
        permission to upload videos — nothing else. Google will warn that the app
        is not verified, because it is yours and nobody has reviewed it; choose Advanced
        and continue. After that you are done, and Pito will not ask again.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="yt-note mono" data-reveal="fade">
      Studio walks you through the same steps with the exact links — the YouTube
      row in the doctor has them ready to copy.
    </p>
  </Section>

